# Core Vibe Coding Workflow

## Purpose
This rule defines the fundamental workflow for phase-based development with test-driven implementation in Spring Boot. Always apply these principles regardless of the specific task.

## Workflow Phases

All feature development follows this structure:

1. **Architect Phase**: Design OpenAPI specification
2. **Phase 0 (Skeleton)**: Create all endpoints with mock responses
3. **Phase 1+ (Implementation)**: Implement each endpoint following TDD
4. **Phase Transition**: Generate summary and document learnings

## Golden Rules

### Always Set Up the Maven Wrapper First
Before any development work:
- Confirm the wrapper exists and runs: `./mvnw -v`
- If missing, generate it: `mvn -N io.takari:maven:wrapper` (or `mvn wrapper:wrapper`)
- Pin the JDK for the project — via sdkman: `sdk install java 21-tem && sdk use java 21-tem`, or via asdf: `asdf install java temurin-21.0.4+7.0.LTS && asdf local java temurin-21.0.4+7.0.LTS`
- Use the wrapper for all Maven commands: `./mvnw clean install`, `./mvnw spring-boot:run`, `./mvnw test`
- Never invoke bare `mvn` when `./mvnw` is present — the wrapper guarantees every contributor and CI runner uses the exact same Maven version
- Add new dependencies to `pom.xml` first, then run `./mvnw dependency:resolve` — never add a jar to the classpath manually

### Always Read Vibe Guides First
Before starting any phase, read the relevant guide from the vibe/ folder:
- vibe_phase_workflow.md - Phase-based development patterns
- vibe_java_spring_boilerplate.md - Project structure and patterns
- vibe_database.md - JPA entity and repository patterns
- vibe_development_lifecycle.md - Git workflow and deployment

### Test-Driven Development (TDD)
- `@WebMvcTest`/`@SpringBootTest` tests MUST be written before implementation
- Tests MUST fail initially (proves they test something)
- Implementation MUST make tests pass
- Tests MUST pass before phase transition

### Code Style Guidelines
Follow the conventions in `rules/300-java-spring-style.mdc`:
- `PascalCase` for classes, `camelCase` for methods/fields, `SCREAMING_SNAKE_CASE` for constants
- Constructor injection via `@RequiredArgsConstructor` — never field `@Autowired`
- DTOs (Java records) in and out of controllers; entities never leave the service layer
- Keep methods focused and single-purpose; extract helpers for complex logic
- Javadoc on all public service and repository methods

### Phase Transition Checklist
Before transitioning to next phase:
- [ ] All `@WebMvcTest`/`@SpringBootTest` tests passing (`./mvnw test`)
- [ ] Compilation clean (`./mvnw compile`)
- [ ] Checkstyle passed (`./mvnw checkstyle:check`)
- [ ] Code formatted (`./mvnw spotless:apply`)
- [ ] Javadoc added to public APIs
- [ ] Phase summary generated
- [ ] Shared libraries documented

## Directory Structure Enforcement

When creating files, follow this structure:
```
src/main/java/com/example/<app>/
├── config/          # Security, CORS, OpenAPI, JPA auditing config
├── web/             # @RestController classes (Experience layer)
│   └── dto/         # Request/response DTOs (Java records)
├── service/         # @Service business logic, @Transactional boundaries
├── repository/      # Spring Data JpaRepository interfaces
├── model/           # @Entity classes
└── exception/       # Custom exceptions + @RestControllerAdvice
src/main/resources/
├── application.yml
├── application-dev.yml
├── application-test.yml
└── db/migration/    # Flyway migrations (V1__..., V2__...)
src/test/java/com/example/<app>/
├── web/             # @WebMvcTest controller tests
├── service/         # Mockito unit tests
└── repository/      # @DataJpaTest / Testcontainers tests
```

## Error Handling

When encountering unknown issues:
1. Document the problem clearly
2. List what has been tried
3. Collect relevant code snippets and the full Java stack trace
4. Generate research-export.md for external review
5. Wait for human input before major refactoring

## Secrets Management

NEVER commit secrets to the repository:
- Use `.env` files (loaded via docker-compose or spring-dotenv) for local development
- Reference `.env.example` for required variables
- Never hardcode credentials in `application.yml` — use `${ENV_VAR:default}` placeholders
- Use environment variables in `docker-compose.yml`
- Validate all required secrets at startup via `@ConfigurationProperties` with `@Validated`

## Response Format

When generating phase summaries or documentation:
- Use clear, professional language
- Include concrete examples
- Reference specific files and line numbers
- List dependencies for next phases
- Note any gotchas or learnings
